  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1251 AM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nA frontal boundary will weaken as it meanders just south of the PAH \nforecast area today into tonight.  Models also show an upper level \ntrof moving over the middle Mississippi and lower Ohio valleys \ntonight into Saturday.  Unsettled conditions will continue with 20-\n60% chances of showers and a few thunderstorms across the region \ntoday, highest chances south, and through tonight, highest \nchances east. On Saturday, 30-70% chances of showers and storms \nwill continue mainly across west Kentucky, southwest Indiana and\nsoutheast Illinois, with the best chances in the afternoon with\npeak heating. \n\nAn upper level high will move over the south central U.S. Sunday, \nbecoming centered over the southeastern U.S. by Tuesday. Our \nregion being under the influence of upper level ridging will \ngive us dry and warmer than normal conditions Sunday through \nearly Tuesday. Models show a surface cold front approaching from\nthe northwest late Tuesday. The front will become oriented west\nto east to the north of our region by mid week, and models \ndiffer on just how far south the cold front will move. The \nmodel blend currently produces 20-40% chances of showers and \nstorms across our northern and western counties Tuesday \nafternoon through Wednesday morning. Chances gradually spread \nsouth across the entire PAH forecast area, peaking at 50-60% \nnorth to 30-40% south on Thursday. \n\nThe coolest temperatures of the next seven days will be today with \nhighs in the 80s.  Winds become southerly late today into tonight, \nand combined with the upper level ridge, temperatures will return to \nabove normal readings over the weekend through at least the middle \nof next week.  Temperatures mid to late next week will be dependent \non just how far south the cold front moves, along with coverage of \nclouds and convection.",
